WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.801
-Thank you so much
for being here, bud.

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.634
I really appreciate this.
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:06.534
-People know you obviously
for being a great singer,

00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:08.200
a great guitar player.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.300
And I don't know if people
know how funny you are.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.067
You also write comedy bits
for "The Tonight Show."

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.968
-I did that one time. I did --

00:00:15.968 --> 00:00:17.968
So, I was up late at night

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:20.834
enjoying an adult Fruit Chew,
let's say.

00:00:20.834 --> 00:00:23.234
-Okay. That makes sense. Okay.
Sure, sure, sure, sure, sure.

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:24.367
-We..
-[ Applause ]

00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:26.100
-This side of the audience
understands.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.300
Yes. Okay. Perfect.
-The band gets it, too.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.100
-Yes.
-And I have all these ideas,

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.367
and there's a part of my brain

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:35.501
that doesn't tell me
that those ideas aren't good,

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:37.300
and they tell me
that I should share them

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.934
with everyone all the time.

00:00:38.934 --> 00:00:41.567
And I wrote a bit
for a commercial for the show,

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:43.868
and I sent it to our dear friend
Andy Cohen.

00:00:43.868 --> 00:00:45.968
-Yeah.
-Just so confident it was great.

00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:48.667
"Send this to Jimmy, please.
Will you send it to Jimmy?"

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.267
And the bit was, "Okay.

00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:52.634
It's a product
called Crypto Bismol,

00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:54.767
and it's for
the stomach cramps

00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:57.667
associated with the fluctuating
price of bitcoin.

00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.300
He's gonna love it!"

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.167
And I never heard
another thing about it until...

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:06.167
until you fully produced
the commercial.

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:07.934
-We made the commercial
and aired it.

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:11.000
Here's a clip
from Crypto Bismol.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.300
-Are you suffering
from heartburn, nausea,

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.834
and an upset stomach

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:17.567
all because you lost
your life savings on crypto?

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.767
Introducing Crypto Bismol.

00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:21.367
Crypto Bismol
may be right for you

00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:22.934
if you suffer from
any of the following

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:24.467
crypto-related illnesses.

00:01:29.501 --> 00:01:32.200
-There you go.
Thank you, Crypto Bismol.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.000
I feel better already.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.434
-Crypto Bismol.

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:37.567
-There you go. That's it.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:38.634
We love it. It was a good bit.

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:40.167
-Thank you.

00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:42.434
-I was so excited
that you did that.

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:44.334
We threw an Easter egg in there.

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:45.934
If you do watch that thing
in the background,

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:47.434
we put one of your albums.
-I saw that.

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:48.868
-You did?
-Yeah. Thank you.

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:51.934
-I didn't know if you saw that,
but we appreciate...

00:01:51.934 --> 00:01:53.667
-And it's only fair
that I offer --

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:56.133
If you have
any song ideas for me...

00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:58.467
-I would never in my life.
-No. Send it over.

00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:01.000
-I want to talk to you
about a couple of music things

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.334
if you don't mind.

00:02:02.334 --> 00:02:04.434
One is, we were here one night.

00:02:04.434 --> 00:02:08.601
I want to say -- The show was
back with an audience, I think,

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:12.567
and it was just right at the end
of COVID, I would say.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:14.801
And Questlove ended up

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.367
playing drums for you
at Madison Square Garden.

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:19.968
-Madison Square Garden, yeah.
This was in spring of last year.

00:02:19.968 --> 00:02:21.534
I was on the Sob Rock tour,

00:02:21.534 --> 00:02:24.868
and the drummer,
Steve Ferrone, got COVID,

00:02:24.868 --> 00:02:26.701
and we had to rethink
the entire show

00:02:26.701 --> 00:02:29.200
and had to do it
without a drummer.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.234
And someone had the idea, like,

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:32.601
"Well, maybe
Questlove is around."

00:02:32.601 --> 00:02:33.701
It might have been Pino
or something.

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:34.901
"Maybe Questlove is around."

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:36.467
Pino Palladino.
Great bass player.

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:39.801
-Pino Palladino is amazing.
-And Questlove came to the gig.

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:41.901
He was coming to the gig anyway.

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:45.133
And somewhere along the line
of him coming to the show,

00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:47.367
he got offered to play
like six songs.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:51.367
And he learned six songs
on his way to the Garden

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:53.601
and ended up on stage playing,
which was --

00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:55.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thank you, again.

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:57.767
-The -- The best --
Wait. Hold on. Hold on.

00:02:57.767 --> 00:03:00.467
The best --
The best reviews, by the way,

00:03:00.467 --> 00:03:03.000
of anything I have read,
was in the "Post" the next day.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.868
All these --
"This was the greatest thing.

00:03:05.868 --> 00:03:08.434
It was the greatest concert."
Do you remember it?

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:11.067
-Okay, so, what is
the exact term that you used

00:03:11.067 --> 00:03:14.200
when you wrote the Bismol --
-Oh. "Adult Fruit Chews."

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.133
-Right. So...

00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:19.400
I was also
on an adult Fruit Chew...

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.501
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:28.300
-...and got to the venue,
ready to enjoy.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.434
This guy is my idol.
Steve Ferrone on drums.

00:03:30.434 --> 00:03:32.968
And I'm ready to enjoy it,
and then they get to me, like,

00:03:32.968 --> 00:03:35.234
[Slowly]
"Yo, can you please drum

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:37.868
for Steve Ferrone, please?"

00:03:37.868 --> 00:03:40.200
-Everything's in slow motion?

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.868
-Everything was
in slow motion.

00:03:41.868 --> 00:03:43.367
Like, yeah.
-But, do you --

00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:46.133
-[Normal voice] It was the most
exhilarating night of my life,

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:47.767
'cause I don't know
how I got through it.

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:49.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
-No, he texted --

00:03:49.434 --> 00:03:52.601
You texted me either that night
or the next day.

00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:54.634
You go, "Dude, I just did
the craziest thing,

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.133
and I think I pulled it off."

00:03:56.133 --> 00:03:57.367
And everybody -- Online.

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.067
Everybody was like,
"This was the greatest..."

00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:00.901
-It was the greatest.
I think it's because

00:04:00.901 --> 00:04:02.734
people get very nervous
around their Garden show

00:04:02.734 --> 00:04:04.100
and they don't really
change it up

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.934
and they sort of, like,
do the safe show.

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:07.801
And there was --
We didn't have the opportunity

00:04:07.801 --> 00:04:09.968
to do anything safe,
and it was a magical night

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:12.701
at the Garden that you wouldn't
have seen anywhere else.

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:14.667
And Questlove is to thank
for the ending,

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:16.667
having some power and energy
playing the drums.

00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:19.968
So thank you very much.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:23.734
-This is the fall leg,
the fall leg of the Solo tour.

00:04:23.734 --> 00:04:25.534
Now, by calling it
the Solo tour,

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:28.267
I want to remind everyone
that it is solo.

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:30.167
It is just --
-Just me alone with

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:32.534
an acoustic guitar on the stage.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:32.534 --> 00:04:34.133
-Seriously.
-Yes.

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:35.501
-But you're doing arenas.

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:39.968
-Arenas solo. And it works.

00:04:39.968 --> 00:04:42.334
It's an opportunity for me
to play some of these songs

00:04:42.334 --> 00:04:44.334
from my career
and tell stories around them

00:04:44.334 --> 00:04:46.501
and read signs
with requests on them,

00:04:46.501 --> 00:04:48.968
and they try to stump me and see
if I remember an old song.

00:04:48.968 --> 00:04:52.067
And it's just very open-ended
and great and fun,

00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:54.334
and I'm never quite sure
where the show is going to go.

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:57.133
And it's just really exciting.
-Yeah, that's wild.

00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:59.167
But did you -- Do you go like,

00:04:59.167 --> 00:05:02.400
"Well, I kind of need the band,
I need bass and drums"?

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.801
-You definitely trade off
the fullness of a band,

00:05:05.801 --> 00:05:10.367
but what you get is instant
turn on a dime with any moment.

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:13.067
Like, the other night,
or a few months back

00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:15.100
when I was doing a solo thing,
I was, like, playing a song.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.934
And I go -- And you
can't do this in a band.

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:19.968
I go, "Boy, I could go right
into Def Leppard 'Hysteria'

00:05:19.968 --> 00:05:21.367
from the end of this."

00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:22.901
And you don't have to
get on a talkback mic

00:05:22.901 --> 00:05:24.133
or think about doing it
tomorrow.

00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:25.434
-There's no click track
behind you.

00:05:25.434 --> 00:05:27.000
-You do it right then and there.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.267
And that's the contract
with the audience, is, like,

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:31.167
you're gonna see me absolutely

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:35.701
in a place like the Garden
just stand out on the tightrope

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:37.467
and play music
on an acoustic guitar.

00:05:37.467 --> 00:05:39.567
And it's the most fun.

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:41.000
It's the most fun
to have finished.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.801
You know the kind of shows
where they're hard

00:05:42.801 --> 00:05:44.701
when you're doing them
and then when you're done,

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:46.667
you're just so rewarded
from having that --

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:48.667
-Yeah, you go, "That was so..."
I know that.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:50.734
-Yeah, it was great.
-I mean, not to an arena,

00:05:50.734 --> 00:05:52.667
but, yeah, a comedy club.

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:54.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:57.033
-I will say I love "Sob Rock."
It came out. I go, "Sob Rock."

00:05:57.033 --> 00:05:59.567
It was like sad rock
or something

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:01.133
or yacht-rock kind of vibe.

00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:03.567
I go, "Okay," and I'm listening
to it and I'm loving it.

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:06.534
And you're doing my favorite
song off the album tonight,

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:08.000
which is
"Shouldn't Matter but It Does."

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.434
-"Shouldn't Matter but It Does."

00:06:09.434 --> 00:06:11.501
-And I will say, if you do
listen to this on Spotify

00:06:11.501 --> 00:06:14.467
or listen to "Sob Rock,"
your phone turns into a Walkman.

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:15.834
-Yes.
-Cassette player.

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:17.267
And the cassette is spinning.
-Yes.

00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:18.901
-And it is rad.
Was that your idea?

00:06:18.901 --> 00:06:21.000
-Thank you. Yes, I am
a detail-oriented guy.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.467
-It was rad. I love it.
Do it tonight.

00:06:23.467 --> 00:06:26.300
-Otherwise, you get the reel
on Spotify

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.734
and you'll be working out
and it's like six seconds long,

00:06:28.734 --> 00:06:30.367
and your brain
will start learning the cuts.

00:06:30.367 --> 00:06:33.400
So it's like person dancing,
person twinning...

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.033
guy running, boom,
person dancing...

00:06:35.033 --> 00:06:37.300
-Over and over again. You go...
"Oh, this is a loop."

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.100
-And I was like,
"If it's going to loop,

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.701
just make a cassette tape
looping."

00:06:40.701 --> 00:06:43.133
And you don't know this,
but in each song,

00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:45.868
the tape inside the cassette

00:06:45.868 --> 00:06:48.801
moves relative
to the other side of the reel

00:06:48.801 --> 00:06:50.968
to the way
it would be in a Walkman.

00:06:50.968 --> 00:06:52.467
-Wow!

00:06:52.467 --> 00:06:54.968
-So the tape is actually
moving to the other side.

00:06:54.968 --> 00:06:56.434
A whole generation of people

00:06:56.434 --> 00:06:58.133
wouldn't understand
that reference.

00:06:58.133 --> 00:07:00.067
-It's really funny.

00:07:00.067 --> 00:07:02.801
You also announced that you're
launching your own SiriusXM

00:07:02.801 --> 00:07:04.100
channel called

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.634
-"Life with John Mayer."

00:07:05.634 --> 00:07:07.334
-Yes.
-On SiriusXM.

00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:09.868
I love the idea of this,
but please explain

00:07:09.868 --> 00:07:11.367
what this idea is,
because I do love it.

00:07:11.367 --> 00:07:15.100
-Okay, the idea is
a radio channel on Sirius

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.934
that isn't genre-based,

00:07:16.934 --> 00:07:19.200
but plays music
for whatever moment

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:20.868
you're most likely to be
in your day

00:07:20.868 --> 00:07:23.100
or week, your month,
or even your year.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.334
As I hear myself say it.
As I hear myself say it.

00:07:25.334 --> 00:07:27.167
And so if you're waking up
in the morning

00:07:27.167 --> 00:07:29.300
and you're on the way to work
and you turn it on,

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.234
it's gonna be high-energy
go-to-work music.

00:07:31.234 --> 00:07:33.801
If you're at work,
it's already sort of meeting you

00:07:33.801 --> 00:07:35.133
where you are in the moment.

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:37.434
Friday nights, going-out music.

00:07:37.434 --> 00:07:39.701
So, like, you'll plug your phone
into the aux cord

00:07:39.701 --> 00:07:41.200
and sound like a genius

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.200
because the music's already
set up for playing in an Uber

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:45.934
on your way to a bar
on a Friday night.

00:07:45.934 --> 00:07:47.501
Saturday night
dinner-party music.

00:07:47.501 --> 00:07:49.467
Sunday night making omelets.

00:07:49.467 --> 00:07:51.901
Sorry. Sunday morning
making omelets.

00:07:51.901 --> 00:07:54.901
You know, there's music
for amorous times.

00:07:54.901 --> 00:07:56.267
-Ooh!
-And I -- I'll be like,

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:57.400
"Hey. What's up?
You're listening to

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:58.601
'Life with John Mayer.'

00:07:58.601 --> 00:08:00.267
You guys are doing great.
Keep it up."

00:08:00.267 --> 00:08:02.200
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:03.734
-You're coaching people --

00:08:03.734 --> 00:08:05.667
You're coaching people along
with whatever.

00:08:05.667 --> 00:08:08.400
-Yeah, yeah.
I'll whisper those I.D.s, yeah.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.267
-When is that coming to Sirius?

00:08:10.267 --> 00:08:12.267
-That's gonna come November.

00:08:12.267 --> 00:08:14.601
-November.
"Life with John Mayer."

00:08:14.601 --> 00:08:17.167
Check that out. Look for that.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:17.167 --> 00:08:19.200
Check out his Solo tour
this fall.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.200
John is performing for us
when we come back, everyone.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:23.467
Stick around! John Mayer!

